TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is forward biased, so it allows current to flow through it.</p>\n<p>Due to this configuration, there is a current flowing through <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.339ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.861ex\" height=\"1.885ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 1264.6 833\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D437\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D437\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(861,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, and a voltage drop of 5 V occurs across the resistor connected in the circuit. As a result, the output voltage (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.72ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.386ex\" height=\"2.265ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 1938.8 1001.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D449\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D449\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(616,-176.7) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\">out</text></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>) is 0 V.</p>","exam":"jee-main","questions":[{"question_id":"pthe-output-voltage-in-the-following-circuit-is-consider-jee-main-physics-3teg5